00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
have you noticed that it is usually  exciting to learn something at the  beginning but because it takes effort  and time you tend to easily give up  there are so many things to learn and  you feel overwhelmed  you've been trying to improve your craft  but then comes the next shiny thing  trying to steal your attention away  and the results  outputs that you are hardly satisfied  with just for the aim of producing  something  if you want to achieve better than  average results you need to step up your  game  the thing is that in order to remain  valuable in our economy you must master  the art of quickly learning complicated  things  this task requires deep work  if you don't develop this ability you  will likely fall behind as technology  advances  in a nutshell if you cannot learn you  cannot thrive  no matter how skilled or talented you  are  have you ever worked on a project that  demands your full focus like debugging  some code writing an article designing a  presentation or building out a strategic  plan  and totally lost track of time because  you were so engaged on completing this  task  this awesome experience has been coined  as a flow state which can be best  described as functioning completely in  the zone  flow states are best achieved during  deep work where you are dedicated and  focused on a single task for an extended  chunk of time and can really get into  the problem at hand  it's in these deep work sessions when we  are the most innovative creative and  significantly more productive  in other words professional activities  performed in a state of distraction free  concentration or deep work can push your  cognitive capabilities to their limit  these efforts create new value improve  your skill and are hard to replicate  opposite of this is what is called a  shallow work which does not create much  new value and often can be performed  when you are distracted  shallow type of work can be writing an  email answering a call cleaning your  office desk or any other quick to do  duty  most people spend their days doing  shallow work  and it's often these low value tasks  that keep us from more important higher  value projects that require deep work  for example have you ever been trying to  focus on a big project and been  distracted by a trivial email that you  felt you needed to respond to  immediately  obviously it is incredibly difficult to  completely remove these types of  distractions from your life especially  in the modern office environment  and there's a major reason why we're  more attracted to these types of tasks  they're quick and easy and give us a  sense of accomplishment  in the long term however they're holding  us back from bigger career and life  goals  so what can you do about it  the thing is that the deep work isn't  about working harder or longer  it's about working smarter  ideally deep work is about transforming  your work days from a random series of  reactive events into a well-oiled  schedule that allows you to prioritize  the highest value tasks  deep work is more than just a set of  good habits it's a skill that needs to  be learned  it's like playing the guitar something  you wouldn't expect to be good at unless  you actually practiced and trained for a  long period of time  it's important to emphasize that because  a lot of people think about deep work  more like a habit such as flossing their  teeth  that might sound scary but it isn't  here are 4 simple steps you can take to  begin a deep work regime today step 1  schedule time for deep work  reclaiming your focus means exercising  control over your schedule  start planning out your weeks and block  out sections of time for deep work  the best way to do it is to alternate  between sessions of deep work and more  menial tasks on a single day  you see the average person has about  four to five hours per day of the focus  time in them and is only truly  productive for about three hours per day  this is why you want to maximize your  most productive time for deep work on  high priority high value tasks while  also reserving time to stay on top of  your shallow work duties  the most important thing to remember is  that you're training yourself to reach a  state of deep concentration so you need  to be disciplined in sticking to your  routine  step two create a distraction-free  environment  there is an element of self-discipline  that comes into play when performing  deep work  as you know distractions come in all  shapes and sizes from phones to  co-workers to kids and you're going to  want to get rid of them all  if you work in an open plan office  consider booking a private room or  working from home if you can  if the internet is a problem for you  switch off your phone and disconnect  your laptop  stick to pen and paper or even go to the  local library if you have to just make  sure you have a clear and calm  environment that facilitates deep  concentration  it also helps to associate a particular  location with deep work for example a  different office or even a completely  different building  step 3 embrace rest and relaxation  deep work isn't just about cutting out  distractions so you can focus on big  projects  it's also about getting a good night's  rest and turning up to work refreshed  and relaxed  besides you need to discover your most  effective way of completing deep work  tasks  maybe you find you flow best with a  couple two hour focused work sessions in  the morning and a long lunch break  before diving into shallow work in the  afternoon  or maybe you prefer shorter  distraction-free sessions and mindful  break intervals to rest your eyes and  drink some water  do what feels best for you and know that  maximizing your productivity starts by  taking care of yourself in order to  avoid complications like mental  exhaustion down the line  remember that it's important to give  your body and brain time to recuperate  step four audit your time spent on deep  work and shallow work  deep work is about more than getting zen  at the office  it's about improving your productivity  especially when it comes to high value  goals  create metrics to measure your  productivity and if you can hold weekly  review sessions to keep track of your  progress  during these regular sessions you need  to ask yourself  how many of my planned tasks did i  complete this week  what habits did i make time for  what was left unfinished on my to-do  list by friday afternoon  regularly auditing your calendar can  help give you quantitative insight as to  where your time is going whether it is  aligned with your goals and priorities  and where it may be getting misallocated  it also can help you pinpoint time sinks  say no to things you don't have time for  and minimize daily distractions so that  you can get more of your task list done  and defend your limited time for the  things that are important to you  by tapping into the magic of deep work  and better managing the boring everyday  shallow busy work you can work to find  greater fulfillment in your skill get  more quality work accomplished in less  time and provide an even better value to  your team  as algorithms and robots take on more  and more of the menial everyday tasks  that keep businesses running  employers and clients are going to be  looking to those who can provide real  value to their bottom line  that means those who have prayed  themselves in the art of serious  critical reflection  it's time to dive into the deep end and  tap into your deep work superman or  superwoman if you don't you'll only be  left behind  if you found this video useful i'd  appreciate it if you smash the like  button and click on the notification  bell  also make sure to subscribe to the  coding tech channel for more videos like  this in the future

